 ],
 "literal": "How great although, the measure of grief is what? / Since it does not have the strength to destroy life, / In the saline wasteland where spurge cactus has grown, / On the funeral pyre of mature firewood kindled in the open space, / Having laid her on the bed of bright fire, / There she passed away, my young wife; / Still I live, what is the nature of this?",
 "translation": "However great it may be, what is the measure of this grief? Since it does not have the strength to take away my life. In the saline wasteland overgrown with spurge cactus, on the funeral pyre of mature firewood kindled in the open space, having laid her on that bed of bright fire, my young wife passed away there. Yet, I still live; what is the nature of this life of mine?",
 "modern": "துன்பம் எவ்வளவு பெரியதாக இருந்தாலும், என் உயிரைப் போக்கும் வலிமை அதற்கு இல்லாததால், இந்தத் துயரத்தின் அளவு என்னவோ? கள்ளிச் செடிகள் வளர்ந்துள்ள களர்நிலப் பறந்தலையில், வெட்டவெளியில் மூட்டப்பட்ட விறகு ஈமத்தின் எரியும் நெருப்பைப் படுக்கையாகக் கொண்டு, என் மனைவி அங்கே மறைந்து போனாள். அவளை இழந்த பின்பும் நான் இன்னும் உயிரோடு வாழ்கிறேன்; இந்த என் உயிரின் தன்மைதான் என்னே!",
 "explanation": "This poem is a moving lament (kaiyaṟunilai) by the Chera king Makkothai on the death of his queen. He expresses his deep grief, wondering why his immense sorrow has not killed him, showing that his grief lacks the strength to end his life. He describes his young wife being laid on the funeral pyre of mature firewood kindled in the desolate, cactus-filled wasteland. While she has departed into the bright flames, he is shocked and ashamed that he continues to live. The stark imagery of the burning pyre in the barren landscape intensifies the tragic feeling of loss and the king's self-reproach.",
